WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.520 --> 00:00:04.423
This is 2D hand-drawn animation.

00:00:04.423 --> 00:00:05.770
[horns toot]

00:00:05.770 --> 00:00:09.360
And this is 3D
computer-generated animation.

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:12.950
But many films actually
fall somewhere in between --

00:00:12.950 --> 00:00:16.400
or what's often referred to as "2.5D."

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:18.750
This is when a variety
of techniques are used

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:22.760
to make an otherwise flat 2D film feel 3D,

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:26.700
with depth, but also with
a unique hand-drawn style.

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:28.935
Animators might use illustration tricks

00:00:28.935 --> 00:00:30.526
like shading, layering,

00:00:30.526 --> 00:00:32.592
forced perspective, and parallax

00:00:32.592 --> 00:00:34.710
on 2D objects to make them appear

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:37.100
as if they existed in a 3D space.

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:39.760
Some films even mix dimensions by adding

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:43.150
3D characters and background
objects to 2D films.

00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:46.630
Disney's "Beauty and the
Beast" was primarily in 2D,

00:00:46.630 --> 00:00:49.900
but the chandelier? A 3D CGI add-on.

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:53.680
And animators created this
jungle entirely in 3D,

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:57.130
so a 2D hand-drawn Tarzan
could swing through.

00:00:57.130 --> 00:00:58.370
"Lion King" was the same.

00:00:58.370 --> 00:01:01.860
This wildebeest stampede was
digitally simulated in 3D,

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:03.695
but Simba was in 2D.

00:01:03.695 --> 00:01:05.361
[Simba gasps]

00:01:05.361 --> 00:01:09.260
And animators are continuing
to push 2.5D further,

00:01:09.260 --> 00:01:10.490
because there are still some things

00:01:10.490 --> 00:01:12.470
that computers can't fully replace,

00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:15.620
like the graphic look of comics
in "Into the Spider-Verse,"

00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:18.820
which incorporated 2D
line work on 3D models.

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:21.750
The latest example of 2.5D innovation is

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:24.840
Cartoon Saloon's 2020 film, "Wolfwalkers,"

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:27.890
which, like "Tarzan,"
created a 3D forest --

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:30.870
only this 3D forest would
never actually appear

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:32.300
in the final film.

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:36.060
A team of nine animators
would ultimately recreate

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:38.680
every single digital frame of these scenes

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:41.803
by hand on thousands of sheets of paper.

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:45.630
Eimhin McNamara: The idea
was that it would not feel

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:49.710
too clinical, too clean,
too, like, artificial.

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:51.090
Narrator: That's Eimhin McNamara,

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:53.650
whom directors Tomm Moore and Ross Stewart

00:01:53.650 --> 00:01:57.090
tasked with creating the
"wolfvision" scenes for the film.

00:01:57.090 --> 00:01:59.370
In these scenes, the
viewer sees everything

00:01:59.370 --> 00:02:01.910
through the eyes of the
main character, Robyn,

00:02:01.910 --> 00:02:04.430
as she transforms into a wolf.

00:02:04.430 --> 00:02:06.537
Eimhin: The main thing with the wolfvision

00:02:06.537 --> 00:02:10.097
that Tomm and Ross wanted
to get on screen was

00:02:10.097 --> 00:02:13.830
they wanted to make it feel
like a heightened perspective

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:15.670
or a heightened experience,

00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:18.370
a heightened-reality version of the film.

00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:19.960
Narrator: The animation team could've made

00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:23.330
a fully 3D film relying
on digital technology,

00:02:23.330 --> 00:02:27.350
but they opted for 2D
hand-drawn animation instead,

00:02:27.350 --> 00:02:29.158
specifically with graphite,

00:02:29.158 --> 00:02:31.316
to mimic art from the 1600s.

00:02:31.316 --> 00:02:33.540
But they decided to use 3D tools

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:35.800
to first plan out the scenes.

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:38.060
Using these tools and virtual reality

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:39.710
in the previsualization stage

00:02:39.710 --> 00:02:42.218
helped create more realistic backdrops

00:02:42.218 --> 00:02:45.019
and mimic the natural
movement of the wolf.

00:02:46.035 --> 00:02:48.100
There were two main settings in the film,

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:49.900
the town and the forest,

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:53.330
both with their own wolfvision
scenes and art styles.

00:02:53.330 --> 00:02:56.470
The town, with its woodblock
print, was easily made

00:02:56.470 --> 00:02:59.320
with more traditional 2.5D techniques.

00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:02.030
Animators worked in After
Effects to place elements

00:03:02.030 --> 00:03:04.060
at various distances from the camera,

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:06.310
creating an illusion of depth.

00:03:06.310 --> 00:03:09.600
This built on a technique
that goes back to 1942,

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:11.980
when Disney shot the
opening scene of "Bambi"

00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:13.960
using a multiplane camera,

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:15.730
which has layers of painted glass

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:18.967
to achieve a similar parallax effect.

00:03:18.967 --> 00:03:20.800
But the forest previs scenes

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:22.858
required a lot more innovation

00:03:22.858 --> 00:03:25.320
and a lot more work to get right.

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:27.270
They were inspired by the look and feel

00:03:27.270 --> 00:03:30.720
of the Studio Ghibli film
"The Tale of Princess Kaguya,"

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:33.690
so these scenes required
a more organic stroke

00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:35.500
with a flowy watercolor style,

00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:38.940
a creative choice you
couldn't really get with CGI.

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:40.456
Eimhin started out with a simple

00:03:40.456 --> 00:03:42.122
hand-drawn sketch of the woods

00:03:42.122 --> 00:03:43.940
and a telescoping effect.

00:03:43.940 --> 00:03:45.665
He used depth maps and Photoshop

00:03:45.665 --> 00:03:47.190
to help create this illusion

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:50.070
and sketch out the frames
by hand one by one.

00:03:50.070 --> 00:03:51.580
But he quickly realized they would need

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:53.426
a more dynamic camera,

00:03:53.426 --> 00:03:56.010
because the wolf wouldn't
just be walking forward,

00:03:56.010 --> 00:03:57.990
it would also be jumping, looking around,

00:03:57.990 --> 00:04:00.948
and even bobbing its head
as it panted and breathed.

00:04:02.354 --> 00:04:04.747
Eimhin: It helps you feel
like you're actually in,

00:04:04.747 --> 00:04:06.600
you know, the head of the character.

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:08.900
Narrator: So the team
experimented with virtual reality

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:11.130
using the headset Oculus Rift.

00:04:11.130 --> 00:04:13.920
They drew and built a 3D forest in VR

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:16.000
with a sculpting program called Medium.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:18.010
This virtual set was then transferred

00:04:18.010 --> 00:04:20.360
to a computer-animation tool Blender,

00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:23.800
where artists painted on
more stylistic 2D elements.

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:25.630
Animators used these rough overlays

00:04:25.630 --> 00:04:28.140
to adjust and refine the 3D model.

00:04:28.140 --> 00:04:30.390
Eimhin: You would basically
draw on top of the 3D models.

00:04:30.390 --> 00:04:33.253
Bake in some line work onto the forms.

00:04:33.253 --> 00:04:34.480
Would still give it kind of a nice

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:36.780
kind of squiggly and
kind of hand-drawn feel.

00:04:36.780 --> 00:04:38.770
Narrator: Other 2D
elements included soldiers

00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:41.140
and rough flames for
the forest-fire scene.

00:04:41.140 --> 00:04:43.510
That way, animators wouldn't
have to guess locations

00:04:43.510 --> 00:04:45.200
for those assets later on.

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:47.650
This 3D model was used
to block out the path

00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:49.530
and speed of the camera movements.

00:04:49.530 --> 00:04:50.938
Eimhin: And we would then start

00:04:50.938 --> 00:04:52.096
moving the camera through it,

00:04:52.096 --> 00:04:53.340
bring it to the directors, go,

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:54.910
"OK, well, how's the movement here?

00:04:54.910 --> 00:04:55.830
Is it looking good?"

00:04:55.830 --> 00:04:57.590
They might go, "Actually,
we should probably

00:04:57.590 --> 00:04:58.948
go a bit slower with this

00:04:58.948 --> 00:05:00.714
so it has a bit more, like, oomph."

00:05:00.714 --> 00:05:03.310
Or, "We should go faster."
Whatever the case may be.

00:05:03.310 --> 00:05:06.690
Narrator: Now, Disney used a
similar method back in 1999,

00:05:06.690 --> 00:05:09.014
when animators created a 3D forest

00:05:09.014 --> 00:05:11.322
for a 2D Tarzan to swing through

00:05:11.322 --> 00:05:13.810
using a program called Deep Canvas.

00:05:13.810 --> 00:05:16.120
But they ultimately kept the 3D model

00:05:16.120 --> 00:05:18.770
and used digital paint in the final film.

00:05:18.770 --> 00:05:21.430
For "Wolfwalkers," the
audience never actually

00:05:21.430 --> 00:05:24.790
sees this 3D model or all
the intricate design work

00:05:24.790 --> 00:05:26.130
involved in creating it.

00:05:26.130 --> 00:05:28.950
The final images were
all eventually covered

00:05:28.950 --> 00:05:31.210
in handmade graphite drawings.

00:05:31.210 --> 00:05:32.757
Eimhin: It's not just about concealing 3D,

00:05:32.757 --> 00:05:35.348
it's more about, like,
how can we build on it

00:05:35.348 --> 00:05:37.530
and make it so it is primarily 2D?

00:05:37.530 --> 00:05:38.960
Narrator: Eimhin printed out each frame

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:42.570
of his 3D sequence on paper
using red ink as a guide.

00:05:42.570 --> 00:05:44.328
Then a team of nine animators

00:05:44.328 --> 00:05:46.944
drew directly over each
of these red images

00:05:46.944 --> 00:05:49.390
by hand with charcoal and pencil.

00:05:49.390 --> 00:05:51.950
The bright red could
easily be removed later

00:05:51.950 --> 00:05:55.030
from the blacks and grays in
the digital-clean-up stage.

00:05:55.030 --> 00:05:58.500
Drawing each frame by hand
is a time-consuming process,

00:05:58.500 --> 00:05:59.900
so to help manage the workload,

00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:02.140
they decided to halve the number of images

00:06:02.140 --> 00:06:03.650
by playing with the frame rate.

00:06:03.650 --> 00:06:07.200
Normally, you'd see 24
different images every second,

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:09.660
but here they drop down to 12.

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:11.840
That way they only had to draw 12 images

00:06:11.840 --> 00:06:13.470
for every second of the scene.

00:06:13.470 --> 00:06:14.590
But changing the frame rate

00:06:14.590 --> 00:06:17.040
can also make the scene appear choppy.

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:19.310
Eimhin: So, we kind of
compensated for that

00:06:19.310 --> 00:06:22.643
by smudging things and actually
using the organic qualities,

00:06:22.643 --> 00:06:24.210
so it is just more like a drawing

00:06:24.210 --> 00:06:26.050
than it is like a camera movement.

00:06:26.050 --> 00:06:28.830
Narrator: Smudging was also
used to create motion blur,

00:06:28.830 --> 00:06:31.400
like in this kitchen scene
with the checkered floor.

00:06:31.400 --> 00:06:34.070
And the hand-drawn style
allowed them to get creative

00:06:34.070 --> 00:06:36.770
with certain elements,
like the smoke of the fire.

00:06:36.770 --> 00:06:39.022
Eimhin: So, the graphite would bleed out.

00:06:39.022 --> 00:06:41.443
It would go to the white of the page

00:06:41.443 --> 00:06:43.080
towards where the fire was,

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:44.140
so you would get a nice kind of

00:06:44.140 --> 00:06:46.880
flickery feeling of the fire,

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:51.370
like almost burning up the
charcoal or such on the paper.

00:06:51.370 --> 00:06:52.980
Narrator: Once all of the
drawings were complete,

00:06:52.980 --> 00:06:56.630
they scanned the approximate
6,000 hand-drawn images,

00:06:56.630 --> 00:06:58.290
digitally removed the red lines,

00:06:58.290 --> 00:06:59.940
and touched up the line work.

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:03.120
They also added additional
layers in the composite stage.

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:05.261
As you can see here in this town scene,

00:07:05.261 --> 00:07:07.276
the various tones and textures

00:07:07.276 --> 00:07:10.320
and the colored wolf scent
are all drawn separately.

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:13.110
These multiple layers
meant much more paper.

00:07:13.110 --> 00:07:14.430
For example, this scene,

00:07:14.430 --> 00:07:17.050
when Robyn wakes up as a
wolf for the first time,

00:07:17.050 --> 00:07:20.363
required about 1,500 printed pages.

00:07:22.290 --> 00:07:23.780
The result of all this hard work

00:07:23.780 --> 00:07:26.270
was an innovative 2.5D style

00:07:26.270 --> 00:07:29.280
that highlights the artists'
skills in drawing by hand --

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:32.410
something a computer alone
wouldn't be able to do.

00:07:32.410 --> 00:07:34.126
Eimhin: It's, again, just
about getting an organic thing

00:07:34.126 --> 00:07:36.084
on screen that looks like people made it

00:07:36.084 --> 00:07:38.510
versus, you know, computer hit render.

